  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research the serial algorithmic replacement. Store the matrix contiguously in row-major order. Maintain N column sums for an S-row strip, find its best S-column window with horizontal rolling sums, then update all columns using the entering and exiting rows. This gives O(N^2) total work and contiguous accesses instead of repeatedly scanning S strided entries. Keep input handling unchanged initially to isolate the algorithmic improvement. Use 64-bit accumulators and initialize the maximum from a real window, not zero.",
        "B": "Research four-core parallelism and locality for the O(N^2) rolling-column algorithm. Divide the N-S+1 output row positions into at most four contiguous bands. Each worker initializes its own column sums, processes its band sequentially, and produces a private maximum for final reduction. Keep hot loops synchronization-free and column updates vectorizable. Measure duplicated initialization, bandwidth pressure and worker startup costs; retain a serial path when these costs outweigh parallel work. Use only threading facilities supported by the unchanged build, and verify that parallelism is actually enabled.",
        "C": "Research buffered input fused with a streaming algorithm. Replace all mixed cin/scanf input with one buffered reader that correctly handles signed integers, arbitrary input whitespace, refill boundaries and EOF. For each input row, compute its N-S+1 horizontal S-element sums. Maintain vertical rolling totals over these row sums, storing expiring rows in an S-row ring; subtract old values before overwriting their slots. Evaluate maxima once S rows are available. This avoids storing the full raw matrix and uses O(N^2) work with O(S(N-S+1)+N) storage. Keep the reader reusable independently so its contribution can also be measured with A and B.",
        "G": "Integrate only correctness-passing programs produced by the research trees. Within the existing budget, compare the best standalone children, C's reader combined with A's serial backend, and C's reader combined with B's parallel backend. Treat C's fused streaming design as a competing complete implementation rather than assuming it combines naturally with parallel bands. Every integration needs fresh correctness and end-to-end timing measurements: gains are not additive. Retain the fastest validated complete program, including an unchanged child or baseline if merges regress. Audit negative-only matrices, cancellation, S=1 and S=N-1. Keep tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target and permissions unchanged; never dispatch on fixture identities or expected answers.",
        "analysis": "This run targets Problem B, maximum SxS submatrix sum. The baseline passes four correctness cases and averages 0.125508 seconds across three timings; input dimensions and phase-level costs are unknown. Its serpentine traversal rescans S elements per shift, often through strided memory. The main opportunity is an exact O(N^2) reformulation, followed by input and memory improvements; four-core ARM64 parallelism needs measurement rather than assumed benefit. Across all trees, preserve every valid input and exact output formatting. Use 64-bit partial/window sums and widen operands before arithmetic: the 32-bit result guarantee does not bound intermediate sums."
      },
      "request_sha256": "235eb385649f62246ff660221f219b2ffa09013ebe1fcc82c59ee8bd2d06c437",
